// @test: differential
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0,1
// @tags: vbc, codegen, aot, byte-array, len, packed, regression
// @timeout: 30000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// len16=16
// len8=8
// @expected-stdout-end
//
// T1192 — `len()` on a packed `[Byte; N]` read the array's OWN DATA and
// called the result a length, at Tier 1.
//
// `[Byte; N]` lowers to `verum_cbgr_allocate(N)`, which returns a
// HEADERLESS block: the pointer addresses the first data byte, so there
// is no `ObjectHeader` in front of it and no length word anywhere in the
// allocation. Every probe in `lower_len` assumes otherwise — the
// slice-cell probe reads word 0, the List arm reads a `type_id` at
// offset 0 and a length at 24, the Pack arm reads 32 — so each of them
// reads this array's contents. The length is known only at the
// allocation site, and that is where it is now recorded.
//
// MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED. Before the fix, Tier 1 answers
//
//     len16=0   len8=0   sum=0
//
// against Tier 0's 16 / 8 / 8. I had written here that `len8` would print
// 0x4141414141414141 — the eight `65` bytes read as one little-endian
// word — and that is NOT what happens: the read answers 0. The prediction
// is kept, struck, because it is the more tempting reading and the next
// person will make it: "a probe that reads data bytes prints the data" is
// only true if the probe gets as far as loading them.
//
// The non-zero fill stays anyway, and now for a reason that survives the
// correction: `len8=0` on a buffer full of 65s cannot be an honest
// answer about ANY array, whereas `len16=0` on a zero-filled one is
// merely wrong. If a future regression prints the data after all, this
// line is where it shows.
//
// WHY A VALUE POLE AND NOT ONLY TIER AGREEMENT. `differential` asks the
// tiers to AGREE, and two tiers agree perfectly when both are wrong the
// same way — the adjacent session lost a day to exactly that this week.
// So the expected stdout above names the numbers. If a future change
// makes Tier 0 also read the data bytes, this spec fails, which is the
// correct outcome and is not what a bare agreement check would do.
//
// FOUND BY `docs/by-example/19-file-io`, which printed
// `write failed: <a 10-digit address>` at Tier 1 and exited 0 — an
// ADDRESS in the position of an error value, with no crash and no
// diagnostic. Its honest output is `wrote 29 bytes to …` followed by
// `total lines: 3`.

mount core.prelude.*;

fn main() {
    // Zero-filled: the plain case.
    let buf16: [Byte; 16] = [0; 16];
    print(f"len16={buf16.len()}");

    // NON-ZERO fill, so that "read the data" and "answer zero" are
    // different answers rather than the same one.
    let buf8: [Byte; 8] = [65; 8];
    print(f"len8={buf8.len()}");

    // THE LOOP CHECK THAT USED TO BE HERE MOVED OUT, and why is worth
    // keeping. It summed `[1; 8]` through `while i < ones.len()` and
    // expected 8 — a length that is usable as a BOUND, not merely
    // printable. With the length fixed the loop now runs, and the sum is
    // still 0, because INDEXING a packed byte array reads 0 at Tier 1.
    // That is a different instruction and a different row (T1220):
    // measured with a literal bound `while i < 4`, so the loop provably
    // runs, `[7; 4]` still sums to 0 while Tier 0 gives 28.
    //
    // Keeping it here would make this spec unfalsifiable in the way its
    // own header warns about: "did the length fix work" and "does
    // indexing work" would share one number, and the row that is fixed
    // could not be told from the row that is not.
}
